NAME
    gitman - interactive manager for local Git working trees

SYNOPSIS
    gitman [directory ...]

DESCRIPTION
    gitman maintains an in-memory set of local, non-bare Git working trees and
    provides interactive menus for selecting repositories and running explicit
    Git actions.

    Each accepted directory is normalized to the physical top-level directory
    reported by Git for that working tree. Different paths inside the same
    working tree therefore become one entry. Distinct linked Git worktrees
    remain separate entries even when they share the same underlying Git
    repository.

    Bare repositories are not supported by this first delivery.

INITIAL DIRECTORIES
    When one or more directory operands are supplied, only those operands are
    used as the initial candidates.

    With no operands, gitman resolves:

        state-path user sys gitman conf

    and reads the optional file:

        <gitman-conf>/repositories

    The file contains one encoded directory pathname per non-empty line.

        \\n     pathname newline
        \\\\     literal backslash

    Every other character is literal. A backslash before a character other than
    n or backslash remains a literal backslash followed by that character.
    This keeps one logical directory per line while allowing pathname newlines
    and literal backslash-n text to round-trip unambiguously.

    Blank lines are ignored. Entries are not shell-evaluated: quoting,
    variables, tilde expansion and comments have no special meaning.

    Relative paths from operands and the configuration file are resolved
    relative to the directory from which gitman was invoked.

    If the file is absent or has no non-empty entries, gitman falls back to:

        .

    If the configuration pathname exists but is not a readable regular file,
    gitman records an error and also falls back to ".".

REPOSITORY VALIDATION
    A candidate is valid only when it belongs to a non-bare Git working tree.

    gitman asks Git for the working-tree top-level, resolves that top-level
    physically, and stores that physical pathname as the repository identity.
    Duplicate identities are ignored.

    Validation errors do not prevent other candidates in the same operation
    from being accepted.

ERROR FOOTER
    The bottom footer contains at most the most recent error.

    When one validation operation contains multiple invalid candidates, their
    failures are combined into one footer message. A later successful operation
    clears the previous error.

FILESYSTEM ACQUISITION
    When no valid repositories are stored, gitman opens a filesystem
    multi-selection menu rooted at the invocation directory.

        Enter       browse a directory
        Space       mark/unmark
        Backspace   parent directory
        Tab         confirm selection
        Escape      exit when the repository set is empty

    Confirmed paths are revalidated as Git working trees. If none is valid,
    acquisition is shown again with the validation error in the bottom footer.

REPOSITORY MENU
    With one or more repositories, gitman shows a single-selection repository
    menu.

        Enter   open selected repository
        a       add repositories with filesystem multi-selection
        r       remove repositories with a multi-selection list
        c       clear all repositories from the in-memory set
        s       save the current repository set to configuration
        e       edit the repositories configuration
        Escape  exit gitman

    Remove and clear affect only gitman's in-memory list. They never remove
    directories or alter Git state.

    Cancelling an add/remove submenu returns to the repository menu.

SAVE CONFIGURATION
    The s action writes the current repository set to:

        <gitman-conf>/repositories

    using the configuration encoding described above.

    If the file is absent or zero-length, it is written directly. If it already
    contains data, gitman first asks whether it may be overwritten; the
    confirmation menu defaults to No. Cancelling or choosing No leaves the file
    unchanged.

    The configuration directory is created lazily when needed. A successful
    save replaces the configuration with the current repositories in menu order
    and clears the current error. A save failure leaves the in-memory repository
    set unchanged and reports the failure in the bottom footer.

EDIT CONFIGURATION
    The e action edits:

        <gitman-conf>/repositories

    with an external text editor selected in this order:

        VISUAL
        EDITOR
        vi

    An empty or unset VISUAL falls through to EDITOR; an empty or unset EDITOR
    falls through to vi. nano is not a required or implicit dependency. To use
    it, set VISUAL=nano or EDITOR=nano.

    VISUAL and EDITOR are treated as executable identities/pathnames, not as
    shell fragments. gitman does not evaluate them as arbitrary shell command
    strings.

    The repository-menu session has already restored the normal terminal before
    the editor is started. gitman creates the configuration directory if needed
    but does not implicitly save the current repository set before editing.

    If the editor exits unsuccessfully, gitman keeps the current repository set
    and reports the editor status in the bottom footer.

    After a successful edit, gitman asks whether to reset the current
    repositories and reload the edited configuration. No is the default.
    Choosing No or cancelling keeps the current repository set unchanged.

    Choosing Yes clears the current set and reloads only entries from the
    configuration file. Entries use the same decoding, Git validation,
    physical normalization and deduplication rules as startup configuration.
    Invalid entries are aggregated into the bottom-footer error.

    Explicit post-edit reload does not apply the startup "." fallback. If no
    valid repository remains after reload, gitman returns to filesystem
    acquisition.

GIT ACTION MENU
    Opening a repository shows a small set of frequent actions followed by
    grouped submenus. Sync + Push is the first root action:

        Sync + Push: git add --all → git commit -m <commit-message> →
            git pull --no-rebase --no-edit → git push
        Status: git status --short --branch
        Diff: git diff
        Pull: git pull
        Commit: git commit -m <commit-message>

        Changes >
        History >
        Branches >
        Remote >
        Stash >

    The grouped actions are:

        Changes >
            Diff staged: git diff --staged
            Add all: git add --all

        History >
            Log: git log --oneline --decorate --graph -n 50
            Show HEAD: git show HEAD

        Branches >
            List: git branch -vv
            Switch >

        Remote >
            Remotes: git remote -v
            Fetch: git fetch
            Pull: git pull
            Push: git push

        Stash >
            List: git stash list
            Push: git stash push
            Pop: git stash pop

    Leaf action labels show the concrete Git command or command template that
    will be executed. The displayed text is descriptive; it is not evaluated as
    shell code. Submenu labels are navigation only.

    Enter runs a leaf action or opens a selected submenu. Backspace and Escape
    return to the parent menu.

    p toggles the output presentation mode in Git action menus. The current mode
    is shown in the menu header and persists for the rest of the gitman session,
    including across nested menus and repositories.

COMMIT
    Commit prompts for one commit-message line on the restored terminal and
    executes:

        git commit -m <commit-message>

    An empty message cancels the action without invoking Git. A terminal input
    failure is reported in the bottom footer.

SYNC + PUSH
    Sync + Push is a composite shortcut for the common publish workflow.

    If local changes exist, gitman first asks for the commit message. An empty
    message cancels before changing the staging area. With a non-empty message,
    it runs:

        git add --all
        git commit -m <commit-message>

    If the working tree is already clean, both steps are skipped.

    It then runs:

        git pull --no-rebase --no-edit
        git push

    git pull performs the fetch and integrates the configured upstream with
    merge semantics; gitman does not add a redundant separate fetch or merge
    step.

    The workflow stops immediately on a failed add, commit or pull. In
    particular, a merge conflict prevents git push from running.

SWITCH BRANCH
    Switch first enumerates local branches with:

        git for-each-ref --format=%(refname:short) refs/heads

    It then presents those branches in a menu. Each selectable branch displays
    the concrete command that would be run, for example:

        Switch: git switch feature/example

    Selecting a branch executes git switch for that branch. Cancelling returns
    to the Branches submenu without changing branch. If no local branch exists,
    gitman reports that condition in the bottom footer.

OUTPUT MODES
    pager
        This is the default mode.

        gitman invokes every Git action with --paginate and selects the RumiAI
        pager through GIT_PAGER=pager. It extends any existing LESS value with:

            -R -+F -+X

        -R retains raw control/color handling. -+F prevents less from exiting
        only because the output fits on one screen. -+X re-enables normal
        terminal initialization/deinitialization so the pager screen is
        restored when the pager exits.

        The pager exit is the acknowledgement step. gitman returns directly to
        the Git action menu and does not show a second "Press any key" prompt.

    terminal
        gitman invokes every Git action with --no-pager. Output is intentionally
        left in the normal terminal.

        Before each command sequence selected from a menu, gitman prints one
        header containing the concrete selected menu label:

            --------------------------------------------------
            <selected-menu-label>
            --------------------------------------------------

        Each separator is exactly 50 hyphen characters. A composite action such
        as Sync + Push prints the header once for the whole internal Git sequence,
        not once for each Git subprocess. Branch switching uses the concrete
        selected branch label, for example:

            Switch: git switch feature/example

        After the sequence stops or completes, gitman displays:

            Press any key to continue...

        A composite action does not add intermediate acknowledgements.

    gitman does not clear the normal terminal or insert arbitrary batches of
    blank lines to separate Git action output.

TERMINAL BEHAVIOR
    The menu session completes and restores its terminal state before gitman
    launches the selected Git command.

    In pager mode the selected pager owns the temporary presentation screen and
    gitman recreates the action menu when that pager exits.

    In terminal mode Git writes directly to the restored normal terminal and
    read-key owns the explicit post-command acknowledgement.

    A non-zero Git command result does not end the gitman session. The action
    menu reports a concise failure in its bottom footer.

CONFIGURATION
    gitman reads the optional repositories file at startup when no directory
    operands are supplied. It changes that file only through explicit
    repository-menu save or edit actions; in-memory add/remove/clear changes are
    otherwise not persisted automatically.

SIDE EFFECTS
    The action tree includes both inspection and mutating Git operations.
    Commands such as git add --all, git commit -m, git pull, git switch,
    git fetch, git push, git stash push and git stash pop have their normal Git
    side effects on the selected working tree and, where applicable, its remote.

    gitman does not hide those operations behind opaque aggregate action names:
    leaf entries show the concrete Git command or command sequence before
    execution. Sync + Push may stage, commit, merge remote changes and push the
    resulting branch.

EXIT STATUS
    0     Normal user exit or cancellation.
    1     Interactive/runtime execution failure.
    2     Invalid invocation.
    127   A required command is unavailable.

DEPENDENCIES
    gitman requires the RumiAI commands menu, pager, read-key and state-path,
    plus an available Git executable. Configuration editing uses VISUAL when
    set, then EDITOR, and otherwise the POSIX vi fallback.

